  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 116 AM CDT Sun Aug 2 2026\n\nModels have the main sfc low splitting and lifting to the northeast \ntoday while the weaker sfc low moves over east KY. Lingering low \ncloud cover today will keep highs down once again and near record \ncold high territory unless we can break out for a few hours. There \nis also the potential for lingering drizzle and/or light showers. \nWhile there is some instability present today, it's doubtful \nanything that forms will be robust enough for thunder. \n\nAt this point, Monday and Tuesday are mainly dry as high pressure \nmoves over the Great Lakes. We'll see clearing conditions and rising \ntemperatures to start the week with highs back to the mid 80s by \nTuesday. High pressure to the southeast will bring back southerly \nflow by Wednesday. A few shortwaves aloft are progged to roll \nthrough on Thursday, Friday, and Saturday leading to some weak \n(~20%) afternoon rain chances. The southeast sfc high and a sfc \nfront positioned to our north should keep these chances fairly weak \nand suppressed. By Friday temperatures are pushing 90 again and into \nthe lower 90s over the weekend. With dew points in the mid 70s (or \nhigher) again by late week, heat indices push into the 100-105 \ndegree range by Sunday, if not sooner.",
